course: dictionary
lesson: 9907
title: Tallish: a bit tall
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you exactly how big something is, and some tell you roughly. The ending -ish is a clue: it means «a bit» or «rather». So tallish means fairly tall, a bit taller than average. «The Giraffe went to tallish trees where the shadows fell all blotchy.» These trees are not the tallest trees around, but they are taller than most.
explain: Look at how -ish changes a word. Tall means high up. Tallish is a little less than very tall, but still more than just short. Smallish means a bit small, and brownish means a bit brown. The word gives a rough size, not an exact one.
explain: Use the context to check. The Zebra went to «little thorn-bushes», and the Giraffe went to «tallish trees». The two places are set side by side, so you can see that the trees are bigger than the bushes, but not giant. Swap your meaning into the sentence: «fairly tall trees» still makes sense, so you have it right.
fact: Tallish means fairly tall; a bit taller than average.
fact: The ending -ish means «a bit» or «rather».
fact: «Tallish trees» are not the tallest trees, but they are taller than most.
fact: Smallish, brownish and reddish all follow the same pattern.
fact: Words ending in -ish give a rough size or amount, not an exact one.
exercise: What does tallish mean? | Thin and bendy | *Fairly tall, a bit taller than average | Very tall, the tallest in the forest | Very short, like a little bush
exercise: In tallish, what does the ending -ish mean? | *A bit, or rather | A very large amount | Under the ground | Not at all
exercise: «The Giraffe went to tallish trees where the shadows fell all blotchy.» Which animal went to the tallish trees? | The Elephant | The Zebra | *The Giraffe | The Lion
test: Tom is smallish for his age. What does smallish mean? | Not there at all | Full of stripes | *A bit small | Very big
test: Which word means a bit brown? | Browny-blue | Brown | Brownest | *Brownish
exam: Mia says the new tree is tallish, but Ben says it is huge. Who is right about the size? | Neither, because tallish means tiny | *Mia, because tallish means a bit tall, not huge | Ben, because tallish means huge | Both, because they are describing the same tree
